# Omega v1.1 - Planner Feature History & Architecture

**Version:** Phase 0 Complete
**Status:** Architecture Frozen
**Last Updated:** August 2026

---

# Purpose

The Planner feature is designed to solve one problem:

> **"Among all my unplanned projects, what should I work on next?"**

The current Unplanned Project tree is excellent for organizing knowledge and projects, but it does not help users decide what deserves attention today.

The Planner introduces a structured prioritization system while keeping the project tree completely independent.

---

# Core Philosophy

The Planner **does not own tasks**.

The Project Tree remains the single source of truth.

The Planner simply stores references to executable leaf nodes and organizes them according to importance.

This separation keeps the knowledge structure independent from execution planning.

---

# Problem Statement

Without planning support, users eventually accumulate many projects.

Example

```
Android
Portfolio
Resume
AI
DSA
College
Gym
Reading
```

Although everything is organized into a hierarchy, users still experience decision fatigue because every project appears equally important.

The Planner exists to reduce that decision fatigue.

---

# Architectural Decision

## Project Tree

Responsible for

* hierarchy
* project organization
* completion
* expected duration
* sessions

Project Tree never stores planning information.

---

## Planner

Responsible for

* prioritization
* ordering
* today's execution queue

Planner never stores project data.

It only references leaf nodes.

---

# Why Only Leaf Nodes?

A parent node cannot be executed.

Example

```
Android
    Architecture
        MVVM
```

The executable work is

```
MVVM
```

Not

```
Android
```

Therefore only leaf nodes may exist inside the planner.

This rule is permanently frozen.

---

# Priority System

Planner consists of five fixed priority buckets.

| Priority | Capacity |
| -------- | -------: |
| Critical |        3 |
| High     |        5 |
| Medium   |        6 |
| Low      |       10 |
| Backlog  |       20 |

Maximum planner size

```
44 Tasks
```

These capacities are intentionally limited.

The objective is to force prioritization rather than allowing users to mark everything as important.

---

# Why Fixed Capacities?

Traditional task managers allow unlimited High Priority tasks.

Eventually users create

```
High

45 Tasks
```

which makes the priority meaningless.

Omega intentionally introduces scarcity.

Whenever a new task enters a full priority bucket, another task must become less important.

This mirrors real-world attention.

---

# Overflow Algorithm

Planner follows cascading insertion.

Example

```
Critical Full

↓

Move Last Critical → High

↓

High Full

↓

Move Last High → Medium

↓

Medium Full

↓

Move Last Medium → Low

↓

Low Full

↓

Move Last Low → Backlog
```

This cascade continues automatically.

The repository owns this entire algorithm.

UI never performs overflow logic.

---

# Backlog Rule

Unlike traditional applications, the Backlog is also limited.

Capacity

```
20 Tasks
```

Reason

The backlog should remain a manageable parking area.

An unlimited backlog eventually becomes another forgotten task dump.

---

# Backlog Overflow

When Backlog is full

```
Backlog (20/20)
```

and another task needs to enter,

Omega does **not** automatically delete an item.

Instead the user is asked to decide.

Example

```
Planner Full

Backlog has reached its maximum capacity.

Remove one backlog task before adding another.
```

User chooses which backlog task to remove.

Automatic deletion is permanently rejected because it could silently lose important information.

---

# Today's Queue

Today's Queue is not another database entity.

Instead it is a subset of Planner.

PlannerNodeEntity stores

```
todayOrder
```

Rules

```
todayOrder == null

↓

Not in Today's Queue
```

```
todayOrder = 0

↓

First task today
```

```
todayOrder = 1

↓

Second task today
```

This keeps the implementation extremely simple.

---

# Planner Database Design

Planner data is completely separated from the project tree.

Current design

```
PlannerNodeEntity

plannerId

nodeId

priority

priorityOrder

todayOrder

addedToPlannerAt
```

Notes

* nodeId references an UnplannedProjectEntity
* nodeId must be unique
* Only leaf nodes may appear
* Today Queue information is stored inside this entity

---

# Why Planner Is Separate

Priority changes frequently.

Example

```
Today

Critical
```

Tomorrow

```
Medium
```

The project itself never changed.

Only its importance changed.

Therefore planning information belongs in PlannerNodeEntity instead of UnplannedProjectEntity.

